{
 "_about": "The one per-language table the i18n tools read (i18n_tool.py; the build's I18n module reads the same file once PR L1 moves it to apps/game/i18n/languages.json and adds the picker names, font chain, reading speed and review state, PLAN.md). Nothing else lists these facts. 'languages' is keyed by Godot locale code: 'glossary' is the language's key in pipeline/glossary.json and the style packet; 'script' picks the script guard; 'family' is the base language, and every file of a family with two or more files must be complete (Godot looks a message up in every loaded file of the same language, so a gap in zh_Hant shows zh_Hans text: DESIGN.md, 'A missing line falls back to its sibling file'); 'plural_forms' is the gettext header rule (CLDR, the same as Godot's core/string/locales.h); 'gender' names a row of 'gender_rules'; 'name_case' turns on the check that no case ending is glued to the name slot (Slavic languages decline names). A gender rule holds these case-insensitive regular expressions: 'forms' matches one word that shows a person's grammatical gender (past tense, agreeing adjective or participle, job noun), 'you' a second-person pronoun, 'first_person' a first-person subject pronoun (a clause with one is about the speaker, WOLF), 'first_forms' (optional) one word that is first person by its ending alone (Polish -łem/-łam: WOLF's own verbs, skipped unless the engineer speaks), 'pronouns' a gendered third-person pronoun (searched in running text, so CJK rules need no spaces). 'extends' copies every field the row does not set from another row. Patterns are heuristics: a flag costs a look, a miss is caught by native review (PIPELINE.md, 'The engineer is never gendered').",
